</ul>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">How much does a packaging conveyor system cost?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>There is no list price for a conveyor. The number on the quote is built up from the factors below.</p>
<p><strong>Key cost factors:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Conveyor type:</strong> gravity roller is the least expensive; spiral and pallet conveyors cost significantly more</li>
<li><strong>Length and width:</strong> longer runs and wider belts increase cost proportionally</li>
<li><strong>Materials:</strong> food-grade stainless steel costs more than painted carbon steel</li>
<li><strong>Controls:</strong> PLC-controlled systems with sensors and VFDs add substantial cost vs. simple on/off</li>
<li><strong>Environment:</strong> washdown, cleanroom, or hazardous-area ratings increase cost</li>
<li><strong>Installation:</strong> structural supports, electrical, and integration with existing equipment</li>
</ul>
<p>The best approach is to contact manufacturers directly with your specific requirements. See our <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">cost factors guide</a> for a detailed breakdown of what drives conveyor pricing.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What type of conveyor is best for packaging lines?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>The right conveyor depends on your product, throughput, and environment:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="/conveyor-types/flat-belt-conveyors/">Belt conveyors</a>: lightweight products, irregular shapes, gentle handling</li>
<li><a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">Roller conveyors</a>: cases, cartons, totes (gravity or powered)</li>
<li><a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a>: zone-based accumulation, low maintenance, quiet</li>
<li><a href="/conveyor-types/tabletop-conveyors/">Tabletop chain</a>: bottles, cans, small cartons at high speed</li>
<li><a href="/conveyor-types/spiral-conveyors/">Spiral conveyors</a>: elevation changes without eating floor space</li>
</ul>
<p>Most packaging lines combine multiple types. <a href="/conveyor-types/">Browse all 30+ types</a>.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What is zero-pressure accumulation?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p><strong>Zero-pressure accumulation (ZPA)</strong> means products queue on a conveyor without touching each other or building up backpressure. Each zone has its own photo-eye sensor and independent drive. When a downstream zone is occupied, upstream zones stop their products with controlled gaps between them.</p>
<p>ZPA prevents product damage, label scuffing, and carton crushing. It's critical for fragile products and high-value items. Most commonly implemented using <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a> or <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">pneumatic accumulation conveyors</a>.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">How do I calculate conveyor speed for my line?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>The basic conveyor speed formula:</p>
<p class="formula"><strong>Speed (FPM) = Products/min × (Product length + gap) in ft</strong></p>
<p>There is no single correct speed for cases, bottles, or pallets. Set the rate from the fastest machine the conveyor must feed or clear, then add headroom so the conveyor can catch up after a stop. Confirm the result against the manufacturer's rated speed for your model and load. See our <a href="/resources/conveyor-design-guide/">design guide</a> for throughput sizing formulas.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">What is the difference between MDR and powered roller?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p><strong>MDR (Motor Driven Roller)</strong> uses small low-voltage DC motors (commonly 24 VDC) built inside individual rollers. Each zone, a short group of rollers sized to the product, operates independently with its own photo-eye and control card. This enables zero-pressure accumulation and zone-by-zone control.</p>
<p><strong>Powered roller (line-shaft or belt-driven)</strong> uses one external motor driving all rollers through a common belt or chain. All rollers spin together, so there's no zone control without additional pneumatic stops or clutches.</p>
<p><strong>MDR advantages:</strong> lower energy use because zones run only when product is present (savings depend on duty cycle), quieter operation, less maintenance (no belts/chains to replace), better product protection. <strong>Powered roller advantages:</strong> lower upfront cost, simpler for continuous-flow applications with no accumulation requirement.</p>

</div>
</div>
